  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions expected \nthrough midweek.\n\nBreezy conditions to continue through sunset this evening. Surface\nhigh pressure builds over the Southern US and winds decrease\nthrough Tuesday. The weak surface high will shift east into the\nAtlantic by Wednesday and southerly flow will increase ahead of\nan approaching storm system on Thursday. Dry and warm, above\nnormal temperatures are expected through Wednesday...with\nwidespread highs Tues/Wed in the 60s and lower 70s. Overnight\nlows remain fairly typical featuring 30s and a few lower 40s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Late week system brings coldest air of the season \nalong with upslope snow potential in the mountains. \n\nA low pressure system develops across the Northern Great Plains\nmidweek and moves into the Ohio Valley on Thursday. A deep\nupper-level trough will accompany the low as it moves into the\nEastern CONUS. The system will bring a chance of rainfall to the\nMid-Atlantic, though models have been trending drier over the\npast several runs. Greatest rain chances will be west of the \nBlue Ridge, with lower confidence in rain coverage for the \nPiedmont.\n\nThe low redevelops near the East Coast on Friday, but again \nmodels differ in where this occurs and slightly differ on timing\nwith some models keeping the low over the Northeastern US, \nwhile others take it out to sea quickly. The location will be \nimportant as to how much wraparound moisture is available for a \nnorthwest upslope snow event in the mountains as the upper- \nlevel trough moves over the area. Current thinking is that some \nupslope snow will be possible in the western mountains, but \nstill too early to determine how much.\n\nStrong cold air advection behind the departing system will keep\ntemperatures across the mountains in the 30s and low 40s.\nUpslope clouds will also limit any warming, so have trended\ntemperatures during the day slightly cooler. Cold overnight \nwith widespread 20s and lower 30s...the coldest being Sunday \nmorning featuring low/mid 20s in the mountains and upper \n20s/low 30s east of the mountains.",
